DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       This program is part of NNeettppbbmm(1).

       ppaallmmttooppnnmm reads a Palm Bitmap as input, from Standard Input or _p_a_l_m_f_i_l_e
       and produces a PPM image as output.

       Alternatively (when you specify  --ttrraannssppaarreenntt),  ppaallmmttooppnnmm  writes  the
       value of the transparent color in the Palm Bitmap to Standard Output.

       PPaallmmttooppnnmm  can  convert Palm Bitmaps with the following features.  This
       does not mean that it doesn't handle other features.   These  are  just
       the ones we found worth mentioning.


       •      Version 0

       •      Version 1

       •      Version 2

       •      Version 3 (new in Netpbm 10.27 (March 2005))

       •      Scanline compression

       •      RLE compression

       •      Packbits compression (new in Netpbm 10.27 (March 2005))



OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       In  addition  to  the options common to all programs based on libnetpbm
       (most notably --qquuiieett, see
        Common Options ⟨index.html#commonoptions⟩ ), ppaallmmttooppnnmm recognizes  the
       following command line options:



       --vveerrbboossee
              Display various interesting information about the input file and
              process.


       --ttrraannssppaarreenntt
              If the Palm Bitmap has a transparent color set, ppaallmmttooppnnmm writes
              the value for that color to Standard Output in the form #RRGGBB,
              where RR, GG, and BB are two-digit hexadecimal numbers  indicat‐
              ing a value in the range 0 through 255.  If no transparent color
              is set in the Bitmap, ppaallmmttooppnnmm writes nothing.  ppaallmmttooppnnmm  does
              not generate any output image when you specify --ttrraannssppaarreenntt.


       --rreennddiittiioonn NN
              Palm  Bitmaps  may  contain  several different renditions of the
              same image, with different depths.  By default, ppaallmmttooppnnmm  oper‐
              ates  on  the first rendition (rendition number 1) in the image.
              This switch allows you to operate on a different rendition.  The
              value  must be between 1 and the number of renditions in the im‐
              age, inclusive.


       --sshhoowwhhiisstt
              This option causes ppaallmmttooppnnmm to write a histogram of  colors  in
              the input file to Standard Error.




